course: dictionary
lesson: 13848
title: New word: jobless
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Jobless is an adjective. It means without a paid job, and it is another word for unemployed. You can find it in the news, in stories and in maths about money. «After the factory closed, my uncle was jobless for six months.» The words after the factory closed tell you why he had no job.
explain: The word the jobless is a noun. It means people who do not have jobs. «The jobless are looking for work again.» Here the jobless are people, not things. A good check is to swap in a phrase: without a paid job makes sense in the sentence, so jobless is the right meaning.
explain: Words in the news often come in pairs. «The jobless rate went up this month.» Jobless rate means the share of people who have no job. When you see jobless figures or left jobless, look for the other words around it to work out what is being counted or what has happened.
explain: A family that is jobless may have money worries. «Mum was left jobless, so we counted every penny.» The worry is in the words counted every penny. Losing a job is usually not a person's fault, and there is help for families when this happens.
fact: Jobless means without a paid job.
fact: Jobless is another word for unemployed.
fact: «The jobless» means people who do not have jobs.
fact: Jobless rate is the share of people who have no job.
fact: Losing a job is usually not a person's fault, and help exists for families who are jobless.
exercise: «After the factory closed, my uncle was jobless for six months.» What does jobless mean? | *Without a paid job | Happy to have a day off | Very tired after work | Working very hard at home
exercise: The news says, «The jobless rate went up this month.» What is the jobless rate? | The hours people spend at work | *The share of people who have no job | The number of pupils in a school | The price of bread in the shops
exercise: «The jobless are looking for work again.» Who are the jobless? | *People who do not have jobs | People on a holiday | Teachers taking a break | Shopkeepers in the high street
test: «Mum was left jobless, so we counted every penny.» What does this sentence show? | *The family had money worries | Mum had started a new job that day | The family had won a prize | The family was moving to a new house
test: Which phrase uses jobless as a noun, for people? | *the jobless | jobless rate | a jobless uncle, who is kind | jobless figures
exam: A family is jobless because the factory closed. Whose fault is it, and what can help? | It is always the fault of the family dog | Their own fault for being lazy | The children's fault for being noisy | *Usually nobody's fault, and help exists
